      "mother": {
        "preparation": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Own the lean sequential dense-DP candidate. Eliminate the n×n vector<bool> and completed-row copy: retain one DP row and represent blockers compactly, preferably sorted/grouped by row so blocked positions are reset without a full grid allocation. Preserve modulo arithmetic, coordinate semantics, n=1, blocked endpoints, and duplicate blocked coordinates. This is the safe fallback for block counts where sparse combinatorics is quadratic; measure it rather than assuming an improvement.",
            "B": "Own an exact sparse-obstacle candidate. For unique blockers, sort by coordinates and use binomial path counts with factorials/inverse factorials through 2n−2, subtracting contributions from coordinatewise predecessor blockers; handle no blockers directly and return zero for blocked origin or destination. Use 64-bit modular intermediates and deduplicate inputs. Its O(b²) cost requires a justified crossover to the dense route, not unconditional selection.",
            "C": "Own investigation of a four-core dense acceleration. The row recurrence is dependent, so do not use an invalid parallel-for over rows: evaluate only correct anti-diagonal/tiled wavefront or segmented-prefix designs with explicit boundary handling and a serial fallback for small work. Focus on ARM64 cache/task overhead and input/layout effects, without changing compiler settings or relying on benchmark-specific answers. Keep any candidate standalone and correct for every valid grid.",
            "G": "Integrate competing candidates only after identical-build correctness and timing evaluation. Do not assume changes compose additively: compare baseline, each tree result, and any adaptive combination, retaining the fastest verified program. Dispatch only from valid input structure (n and unique blocker count), preserve the single integer output and modulo semantics, and retain a robust dense fallback. Check endpoint blocks, n=1, zero/duplicate/aligned blockers, and overflow; do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside the selected integration.",
            "analysis": "The active source is Problem D. The only measured baseline is correct on 4 cases at 0.080387 s mean on 4-core ARM64. It performs Θ(n²) work, allocates a Θ(n²) blocked grid, and copies each DP row; this cannot scale to the stated n bound, while a pure sparse inclusion-exclusion method can itself be Θ(b²). The research plan therefore keeps complementary dense and sparse exact algorithms and lets integration measure a structure-based adaptive choice. No test-value specialization or expected-output substitution is permitted."
